      "raw_time": false,
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
      "body": "Here's how it's wired, then the fix.\n\n**Structure:** `flare_Dataset.__init__` builds `self.df_valid_indices` by taking every Surya timestamp available in `train_data_path` (2010\u20132024) and matching each one *forward* to the closest flare in `hek_flare_catalog.csv` within `ds_time_tolerance` (4 days). It dedupes to one row per flare event, sorts by `ds_index` (the flare's `peak_time`) ascending, then truncates to `max_number_of_samples` \u2014 currently hardcoded to `6` in the notebook's dataset-construction cell (`70e9c5b2` / `204726e0`). `__getitem__(idx)` just does `self.df_valid_indices.iloc[idx]`.\n\nSo `idx=0` isn't \"the June 2010 flare\" specifically \u2014 it's whatever the *chronologically earliest* matched flare happens to be, and since the pool is capped at 6, you never even see anything past the first few weeks of 2010. The 2012 X-class flares are still in `df_valid_indices` conceptually, they're just past position 6, so they get sliced off before `__getitem__` can reach them.\n\n**Fix \u2014 widen the cap, find the row, use that idx:**\n\n```python\n# rebuild with no cap (or a big one) just to locate the row you want\ntrain_dataset = flare_Dataset(\n    index_path=cfg.data.train_data_path,\n    time_delta_input_minutes=cfg.data.time_delta_input_minutes,\n    time_delta_target_minutes=cfg.data.time_delta_target_minutes,\n    n_input_timestamps=cfg.model.time_embedding.time_dim,\n    rollout_steps=cfg.rollout_steps,\n    channels=cfg.data.channels,\n    drop_hmi_probability=cfg.drop_hmi_probability,\n    use_latitude_in_learned_flow=cfg.use_latitude_in_learned_flow,\n    scalers=scalers,\n    phase=\"train\",\n    s3_mode=cfg.data.s3_mode,\n    s3_storage_options={\"anon\": cfg.data.s3_anon},\n    s3_cache_dir=cfg.data.s3_cache_dir,\n    return_surya_stack=True,\n    max_number_of_samples=None,          # no cap while exploring\n    ds_flare_index_path=cfg.data.flare_index_path,\n    ds_time_column=cfg.data.ds_time_column,\n    ds_time_tolerance=cfg.data.ds_time_tolerance,\n    ds_match_direction=cfg.data.ds_match_direction,\n)\n\nimport numpy as np\nmask = train_dataset.df_valid_indices['GOES_class'].str.startswith('X')\npositions = np.where(mask.values)[0]\nprint(train_dataset.df_valid_indices.iloc[positions][['GOES_class', 'ds_index']])\n\nidx = positions[0]      # or pick the specific X-class row you want, e.g. the X5.4\nitem = train_dataset.__getitem__(idx)\n```\n\nThat should surface the 2012 events (X1.7, X5.4, X1.3, X1.1, X1.4, X1.8) we found earlier, each with its own `idx`. Once you know which position you want, you can re-cap `max_number_of_samples` to something small again (e.g. `idx + 1`) for faster iteration, or just leave it `None` for now since this is the TESTING notebook.\n\nNote: `ds_time_tolerance: 4d` and `ds_match_direction: forward` mean the matched Surya anchor timestamp will be up to 4 days *before* the flare's peak \u2014 so `item['ds_index']` won't be exactly the peak time, same as what you saw with the M2.0 example.",
