    {
      "time": 1789497420.3458862,
      "id": "8376775f-9576-4589-99c5-c951b1c7375a",
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
      "type": "msg",
      "raw_time": false,
      "mentions": [],
      "body": "That code isn't a file edit \u2014 it's meant to go in the notebook, replacing the \"Initialize class with Surya stacks\" cell (`70e9c5b2`, or the flipped variant `204726e0` if you're past that point). No changes needed to `flare_dataset.py` or the config.\n\nFor a true one-off, you don't even need to touch the reusable pattern \u2014 just re-run that one cell with `max_number_of_samples=None` instead of `6`, then grab an X-class row directly:\n\n```python\n# rerun the existing \"Initialize class\" cell, just change max_number_of_samples=None\n\nx_class_mask = train_dataset.df_valid_indices['GOES_class'].str.startswith('X')\nidx = np.where(x_class_mask.values)[0][0]   # first X-class match; change [0] to pick another\n\nitem = train_dataset.__getitem__(idx)\nprint(train_dataset.df_valid_indices.iloc[idx][['GOES_class', 'ds_index']])\n```\n\nThat's it \u2014 three lines after re-running the init cell. `max_number_of_samples=None` just means \"don't truncate,\" so `df_valid_indices` holds every matched flare and you can pick whichever `idx` lands on an X-class row. Once you've got the `idx` you want, everything downstream in the notebook (`item['ts']`, plotting cells, etc.) works unchanged since they all just use `item` and `train_dataset`."
    },
