    {
      "body": "Good instinct to double-check \u2014 and there's a real subtlety here that's easy to miss: the labels in your plot (`anchor_time = item['ds_index']`) are **not** necessarily the same timestamp used to actually load the images.\n\nHere's why: `flare_Dataset.__getitem__` loads frames using `self.valid_indices[idx]` (the *Surya* timestamp), but separately stores `item['ds_index']` as the *flare catalog's* `peak_time` \u2014 matched to that Surya timestamp within a 4-day tolerance (`ds_match_direction: forward`). Those two times are usually close but not identical. Your plotting code assumes they're the same thing.\n\n**Two checks, cheapest first:**\n\n**1. Are the three panels numerically distinct at all?**\n```python\nprint(\"input t0 vs input t1 identical?\", np.array_equal(item['ts'][:, 0], item['ts'][:, 1]))\nprint(\"input t1 vs forecast identical?\", np.array_equal(item['ts'][:, 1], item['forecast'][:, 0]))\n```\nIf either prints `True`, you're looking at the same cached array reused, not a genuine third frame \u2014 that would be a bug.\n\n**2. Confirm the real timestamps and source files.**\nThis bypasses `ds_index` entirely and asks the dataset directly what it loaded:\n```python\nreference_timestep = train_dataset.valid_indices[idx]\nprint(\"reference_timestep (actually used to load frames):\", reference_timestep)\nprint(\"ds_index (flare catalog label, may differ):\", item['ds_index'])\n\ninput_offsets = cfg.data.time_delta_input_minutes          # [-120, -60]\ntarget_offset = cfg.data.time_delta_target_minutes          # 0\n\ntrue_timesteps = [reference_timestep + pd.Timedelta(minutes=m) for m in input_offsets]\ntrue_timesteps.append(reference_timestep + pd.Timedelta(minutes=target_offset))\n\nfor ts in true_timesteps:\n    print(ts, \"->\", train_dataset.index.loc[ts, \"path\"])\n```\nEach printed line is the actual S3 filename that was opened for that frame \u2014 the filename itself encodes the timestamp (e.g. `20120307_0002.nc`). If you get three distinct filenames with three distinct embedded timestamps, matching the three offsets, that's solid proof three different physical files were loaded.\n\nIf `reference_timestep` differs noticeably from `item['ds_index']`, your plot's frame labels are off by that gap \u2014 worth switching the plotting cell to compute `anchor_time = train_dataset.valid_indices[idx]` instead of `item['ds_index']` for accurate labels (keep `ds_index` only for reporting which flare this sample corresponds to).",
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
